#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Scripted lane lifecycle for orchestration (audit 2026-10-21, barrier B4; throughput #446).

  lane start <id> --ticket N --claims GLOB... [--base REF] [--after ID...] [--create]
  lane next [--start] [--base REF]        the first ready entry of the ranked queue: its brief header;
                                          --start registers it as lane start would
  lane adopt <id> <worktree> <branch>     register the worktree an Agent created
  lane check <id>                         in the lane: the checks the tree's verification graph selects for
                                          the lane's diff -> receipt for the tree, reusing each check that
                                          passed for an earlier tree of the lane and that no change since
                                          selects; each red check reruns alone on the lane and on its base and
                                          is classified regression, pre-existing or flaky-under-load (#568);
                                          a red cheap check stops it before any heavier check starts and
                                          reruns on the base only; the heavier checks run when it is
                                          pre-existing there (#639)
  lane land <id> [--into WORKTREE] [--keep]
                                          merge --no-ff after a current green receipt, start the lane review
                                          in the background, run the post-merge smoke, and after a green one
                                          finish the lane as done does once its review is clean or marked
  lane review <id>                        the lane's one independent review (#570): Sol 6.1 medium while
                                          Codex has 15% of its weekly limit left, else Opus 5.5 medium
  lane review <id> --mark N fixed:<merge>|rejected:<reason>
                                          mark a finding; the last mark finishes the landed lane
  lane review <id> --fix-lane             register <id>-review-fixes on the landed lane's worktree,
                                          claiming the files the findings name
  lane done <id>                          after landing: stop what the lane left running in its worktree (#661),
                                          remove worktree, branches, targets, scratch
  lane promote <sha> [--ship] [--no-push] the batch gate: the full gate on <sha> unless its tree is
                                          stamped; then, once every lane landed since dev has a finished
                                          review with each finding marked, fast-forward local dev (and
                                          main) to it and push <sha>:dev (and <sha>:main, in one atomic
                                          push) through the pre-push gate, then run the check of
                                          tools/ui-libs.py, which may queue a Sid-Kit and Data Grid pin lane
                                          (#588); --ship refuses a tree that still
                                          has release-notes/unreleased/, runs the perf suite once after the
                                          gate (a red one refuses the ship and lists the lanes since main),
                                          and starts one overall review of origin/main..<sha>, whose findings
                                          become one ticket; --no-push moves the local branches only to a
                                          SHA origin already has (#666)
  lane sync [--drop-local]                fetch, then fast-forward local dev and main to origin under the
                                          landing lock; a branch with commits origin lacks is refused, and
                                          --drop-local resets it to origin after keeping them on
                                          backup/<branch>-<date>[-N] (#666)
  lane perf <sha>                         the perf suite (#579) on <sha> in the gate worktree, by hand: the
                                          timing budgets alone on the machine (verify.sh --suites perf holds
                                          every slot); promote --ship runs it once before main moves
  lane promote <sha> --install [--deploy <host>]
                                          after the promotion, each step only after the previous
                                          succeeded: install on this Mac from the gate worktree,
                                          check-installed --expect <sha>, and with --deploy the Link
                                          package build and deploy.sh <host> (#569)
  lane list [--all] [--json]              --json: each record with its worktree (null until adopt) for scripts;
                                          the text names how far local dev and main are ahead of origin

start prints the brief header for the lane's prompt. It ends with the rule snippets in
tools/agent-hooks/rules/ whose path globs overlap the lane's claims.

Statuses: planned (registered), active (has a worktree), landed (merged), released (merged with a
green smoke while its agent still locks the worktree), done (cleaned up). Only planned and active
lanes count toward the lane cap and hold their claims.

The lane cap is a lane per 2 cores and per 4 GB of physical memory beyond 8 GB, whichever is fewer
(#572); STRAYLIGHT_LANE_CAP overrides it, and start logs the cap with its source. start refuses while
load15 is above 1.5 x the cores. With STRAYLIGHT_LOAD_WAIT=1 (off by default, owner decision on the
#572 review) each verify run (check, the post-merge smoke, the batch gate) also waits until it is not,
at most STRAYLIGHT_LANE_LOAD_WAIT seconds. Its receipt records whether it waited and the load averages
at the run's start and end.

land holds its lock only for the merge, so another land waits for that alone (#573). After the merge,
land runs the smoke that the merged tree's tools/verification_plan.py defines (everything builds,
structure, tooling and fast checks; 1-3 jobs by load) on its own checkout detached at the merge, the
first free <main checkout>/.claude/worktrees/smoke-<n>, so smokes of several lands overlap while the
integration worktree moves on; a merge whose plan selects only the structure checks runs that plan
instead. The lane's green receipt stands for what it verified (#640): a merge whose tree is the receipt's
runs nothing more, and otherwise only the checks the diff from the lane tip to the merge selects, plus
every smoke check the receipt does not show passed. The full gate runs once per batch, at promote, in the gate
worktree <main checkout>/.claude/worktrees/batch-gate, which one promote at a time holds from its
checkout to the end of the gate. A red batch gate lists the lane merges since the last green
verified tree, the bisect list, and moves nothing.

The lane review (#570) gets the lane's diff against the last integration commit it merged, its
ticket and CODING_STANDARDS.md, never the lane's report, in a checkout detached at the lane tip.
It asks `codex app-server` for the weekly limit and runs `codex exec` with gpt-6.1-sol in a
read-only sandbox; under 15% left, a reached limit, no Codex CLI, a failed Sol run or an empty
answer runs `claude -p` with Opus 5.5 instead. The CLI is STRAYLIGHT_LANE_CODEX, else
CODEX_CLI_PATH (environment or $CODEX_HOME/config.toml), else codex on PATH; claude is
STRAYLIGHT_LANE_CLAUDE or claude on PATH. Its receipt, <id>.review.json, holds the reviewer, model,
effort, the limit left, the duration and the findings with their marks. A lane that changes only
*.md and docs/**, none of them a steering file (CODING_STANDARDS.md, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .agents/,
.claude/, tools/agent-hooks/rules/), and a fix lane are exempt. A lane lands onto dev or main only
with a finished review, since promote holds only lanes not yet in dev. STRAYLIGHT_LANE_REVIEW_START=manual makes land and promote --ship print
the review command instead of starting it; promote still waits for the review.

The queue, queue/queue.toml beside the lane state directory (STRAYLIGHT_LANE_QUEUE replaces it),
ranks [[lane]] entries: ticket, id, claims, optional after (lane ids that must have landed) and
optional blocked (an owner question or hardware). next picks the first entry that is not blocked,
not registered yet, whose after lanes have landed and whose claims overlap no planned or active
lane. tools/idle-watchdog asks it every 10 minutes. An entry's optional brief is printed after its header.

State: one JSON file per lane in ~/.local/state/straylight-orchestration/lanes/
(STRAYLIGHT_LANE_STATE replaces the directory), receipts beside it, and every
action and override in ledger.log. Refusals name the rule; `--force-reason`
overrides a cap or a missing receipt and is logged.

land, promote and sync hold the landing lock, <git common dir>/landing.lock, while
they move refs, and give git their LANDING_TOKEN: .githooks/reference-transaction lets
an agent session move main or dev only with it (B2), and its refusal names promote,
sync and sync --drop-local. promote and sync work in any repository they run in, so
Sid-Kit and Data Grid promote and sync with them too; a tree without verify.sh and
tools/verified-tree.sh gets no batch gate from promote.

Claims are globs relative to the repository: `*` and `?` stay within a path
segment, `**` spans segments, and a claim without wildcards covers that path
and everything under it. Two lanes' claims overlap when some path matches both. check adds each path
the lane changed outside its claims to them (#574), and warns only when one is in the claims of
another planned or active lane that is not declared in sequence with it, naming that lane.
"""
